1
00:00:00,250 --> 00:00:03,990
If you love games, what do the people who make them
actually do?

2
00:00:04,130 --> 00:00:08,510
Behind every game screen are people responsible for
very different kinds of work.

3
00:00:08,710 --> 00:00:13,650
Someone defines the rules of play, and someone else
makes those rules work for real.

4
00:00:13,730 --> 00:00:18,090
Others draw the world, build it in three dimensions,
and give it movement.

5
00:00:18,290 --> 00:00:24,310
Today we will focus on game designers, programmers,
2D artists, and 3D artists.

6
00:00:24,510 --> 00:00:27,330
We will see what each group makes, who they work
with,

7
00:00:27,370 --> 00:00:29,990
and which fundamentals are useful at the start.

8
00:00:30,030 --> 00:00:33,710
Job names and boundaries vary between companies and
projects.

9
00:00:33,810 --> 00:00:38,730
Think of these categories as a map for finding your
preferred work, not as a fixed answer sheet.

10
00:00:39,633 --> 00:00:43,953
Imagine a player discovering and opening a treasure
chest.

11
00:00:44,093 --> 00:00:48,993
A designer decides why the player should want to
open it and what reward it should contain.

12
00:00:49,253 --> 00:00:54,098
A programmer makes the lid open and the reward
appear when the player gives an input.

13
00:00:54,138 --> 00:01:00,057
A 2D artist designs the chest's appearance, icons,
and visual information on screen.

14
00:01:00,097 --> 00:01:05,673
A 3D artist builds the chest in space and connects
its materials and motion to the game.

15
00:01:05,713 --> 00:01:11,573
It is a short moment for the player, but a meeting
point for several people's work.

16
00:01:11,953 --> 00:01:16,673
One person may cover several roles on a small team,
while a large team may split them further.

17
00:01:17,093 --> 00:01:22,573
No role sits above the others; they solve different
problems together.

18
00:01:23,733 --> 00:01:28,933
A game designer shapes what the player sees,
chooses, learns, and feels.

19
00:01:29,113 --> 00:01:33,313
A systems designer may create combat rules or
progression structures.

20
00:01:33,453 --> 00:01:39,813
A level designer works with spaces and routes, while
UI and UX designers shape interaction flows.

21
00:01:40,053 --> 00:01:42,713
The job does not end with having an idea.

22
00:01:42,753 --> 00:01:45,533
Designers communicate it through documents, tables,
simple diagrams,

23
00:01:45,573 --> 00:01:48,093
and prototypes so the team can build the same thing.

24
00:01:48,193 --> 00:01:52,193
They play the result, check whether the intended fun
appears, and tune the values.

25
00:01:52,233 --> 00:01:59,033
Core skills include analyzing games, writing
clearly, and listening to other people.

26
00:01:59,373 --> 00:02:04,473
Spreadsheets and basic engine skills also help
designers test ideas quickly.

27
00:02:04,693 --> 00:02:08,113
A strong designer is less a person who dictates
answers

28
00:02:08,153 --> 00:02:10,673
and more a person who makes the team's problem
clear.

29
00:02:11,750 --> 00:02:16,790
Game programmers implement systems so design and art
can function inside the game.

30
00:02:17,030 --> 00:02:20,950
Gameplay programmers may build movement and combat.

31
00:02:21,190 --> 00:02:26,410
Others specialize in graphics, engines, physics,
tools, networking, or servers.

32
00:02:26,830 --> 00:02:32,770
For a chest, the programmer connects input, opening
conditions, and reward storage.

33
00:02:33,030 --> 00:02:36,090
They trace and fix incorrect or slow behavior, and
may

34
00:02:36,130 --> 00:02:38,370
build tools that help the rest of the team work.

35
00:02:38,410 --> 00:02:44,010
At the beginning, learning program flow through one
language such as C++ is a useful start.

36
00:02:44,490 --> 00:02:50,130
Data structures, algorithms, math, and debugging
remain useful foundations as problems grow.

37
00:02:50,450 --> 00:02:53,670
But the important point here is not how many syntax
rules you know.

38
00:02:54,170 --> 00:02:57,270
A strong programmer understands the game's intent

39
00:02:57,310 --> 00:02:59,850
and produces behavior the team can trust.

40
00:03:01,267 --> 00:03:06,393
2D artists show the mood and shapes of a game's
world visually.

41
00:03:06,553 --> 00:03:12,133
Concept artists explore characters and environments
and create visual guides for the team.

42
00:03:12,273 --> 00:03:16,333
Depending on the project, artists may handle
character illustrations,

43
00:03:16,373 --> 00:03:18,533
backgrounds, icons, interfaces, or sprite animation.

44
00:03:18,713 --> 00:03:23,973
The work is not only about making a pretty picture;
gameplay information must also read clearly.

45
00:03:24,133 --> 00:03:26,453
They check whether enemies and allies are distinct
and whether

46
00:03:26,493 --> 00:03:28,373
important objects disappear into the background.

47
00:03:28,413 --> 00:03:31,713
Useful fundamentals include observation, simple
forms,

48
00:03:31,753 --> 00:03:33,233
proportion, silhouette, composition, value, and
color.

49
00:03:33,473 --> 00:03:38,293
Artists create several quick directions and revise
them repeatedly through feedback.

50
00:03:38,633 --> 00:03:41,113
They must consider how an image will look and
function inside

51
00:03:41,153 --> 00:03:43,180
the actual game screen, not only as a finished
illustration.

52
00:03:43,220 --> 00:03:47,313
2D art designs both a game's first impression and
the information the player needs.

53
00:03:48,710 --> 00:03:54,717
3D artists create characters, environments, and
props that can be viewed from every angle.

54
00:03:54,917 --> 00:04:00,517
Modelers create form, while texture and material
work defines surface color and feel.

55
00:04:00,637 --> 00:04:05,577
Rigging and animation prepare characters and objects
to move naturally.

56
00:04:05,717 --> 00:04:11,237
Environment artists arrange assets and lighting to
complete a space.

57
00:04:11,337 --> 00:04:17,097
Games require artists to consider both visual
quality and whether an asset moves and renders well.

58
00:04:17,137 --> 00:04:21,917
They repeatedly check scale, orientation, movement,
and readability inside the engine.

59
00:04:22,317 --> 00:04:27,697
Useful fundamentals include form, proportion,
spatial thinking, light, and materials.

60
00:04:27,957 --> 00:04:30,837
It is usually better to finish a small prop such as
a

61
00:04:30,877 --> 00:04:33,177
box or chair before attempting a complex character.

62
00:04:33,217 --> 00:04:39,677
3D art does not stop at turning a drawing into a
model; it connects the asset to a playable space.

63
00:04:40,700 --> 00:04:44,520
We used four broad categories, but real teams have
many more specialized jobs.

64
00:04:44,740 --> 00:04:49,620
Design can include gameplay, systems, levels,
interfaces, and user experience.

65
00:04:49,780 --> 00:04:55,440
Programming can include gameplay, engines, graphics,
networking, tools, and artificial intelligence.

66
00:04:55,600 --> 00:05:01,440
Art can include concept, character, environment,
animation, visual effects, and technical art.

67
00:05:01,640 --> 00:05:05,620
The same job title may involve different work
depending on the company and genre.

68
00:05:05,980 --> 00:05:08,480
You do not need to memorize every specialization at
the beginning.

69
00:05:08,520 --> 00:05:13,260
Make something small and notice which kinds of
problems are the most interesting to you.

70
00:05:13,420 --> 00:05:15,980
Trying the whole pipeline once and then choosing one

71
00:05:16,020 --> 00:05:18,800
area to study deeply is a practical starting point.

72
00:05:19,833 --> 00:05:25,233
Production is not a sequence where design,
programming, and art each finish once and close.

73
00:05:25,413 --> 00:05:29,533
A designer defines a rule, then a programmer creates
a form that can be tested quickly.

74
00:05:29,753 --> 00:05:34,913
When art assets arrive, the team checks visuals and
motion and finds problems through playtesting.

75
00:05:35,273 --> 00:05:38,933
The results lead to more changes in rules, code,
placement, and art.

76
00:05:39,153 --> 00:05:41,553
Reducing wasted iteration requires the ability to
explain

77
00:05:41,593 --> 00:05:44,253
your work and understand other roles' constraints.

78
00:05:44,293 --> 00:05:47,513
When something breaks, it is more useful to show
what changed

79
00:05:47,553 --> 00:05:50,133
in which situation than to look for someone to
blame.

80
00:05:50,573 --> 00:05:53,173
Solo developers cannot skip this process either;
they

81
00:05:53,573 --> 00:05:56,013
simply change hats and ask the same questions alone.

82
00:05:56,573 --> 00:05:59,473
Game development combines skill in each discipline
with collaboration

83
00:05:59,513 --> 00:06:01,753
that connects every result into one playable
experience.

84
00:06:02,817 --> 00:06:08,157
If you do not know which role fits you, touch one
tiny scene in four different ways.

85
00:06:08,357 --> 00:06:13,157
If design interests you, summarize a familiar game's
rules on one page and change one of them.

86
00:06:13,537 --> 00:06:17,800
If programming interests you, make a character move
and interact with one object.

87
00:06:17,840 --> 00:06:23,289
If 2D art interests you, create a small character or
icon set and check it at actual screen size.

88
00:06:23,329 --> 00:06:27,937
If 3D art interests you, model a small prop and
place it inside an engine.

89
00:06:27,977 --> 00:06:34,037
A huge first project matters less than finishing a
small result and explaining your contribution.

90
00:06:34,297 --> 00:06:37,277
Record which problems felt exciting and which felt
frustrating,

91
00:06:37,317 --> 00:06:39,597
and your preferred role will become clearer.

92
00:06:39,637 --> 00:06:42,177
Loving games is a good starting point. Now discover

93
00:06:42,217 --> 00:06:45,017
how you want to use that interest to make them.

94
00:06:45,137 --> 00:06:49,297
Which would you like to try first: design,
programming, or art?
